title: Footnotes
order: 7
---
# Footnotes

::marks

::::plate[A reference and a definition](mark="[^1]", tone=link)
A footnote is a reference in the text and a definition anywhere in the document:

```md result
A claim[^1], and a second one[^smith].

[^1]: The source, with a [link](https://example.com).
[^smith]: Smith, 2020.
```

:::notes
The reference renders as a number that links to the note, and the notes are collected at the end of the document, in the order they are first referred to.

On the atlas: [footnote](/atlas#footnote).
:::
::::

::::plate[A label](mark="[^a]", tone=link)
A label is any run of characters without whitespace, brackets or `^`. Labels match exactly, case included.

:::notes
The number a reader sees comes from the order of the references, not from the label, so `[^smith]` is as good a label as `[^2]`.
:::
::::

::::plate[A definition](mark="[^1]:", tone=link)
A definition is a line that starts with `[^label]:` and has text after it, or text on the lines below indented by four spaces, which is how Prettier writes a long one. It may hold links, emphasis, code and references to other notes.

:::notes
It can be written anywhere: under the paragraph that cites it, at the end of the document, inside a block quote or a list item. Wherever it is, it renders in the section at the end.
:::
::::

::::plate[A longer note](mark="····", tone=link)
A longer note continues as a list item does. After a blank line, a block indented by four spaces belongs to the note, and the first line that is not blank and not indented ends it:

```md score
[^1]: The source.

    A second paragraph, and a list:

    - one
    - two

Not part of the note.
```
::::

::::plate[Referring to a note twice](mark="[^1]", tone=link)
Referring to a note twice gives both references the same number, and the note a link back to each.
::::

::::plate[The id of a note](mark="#fn-", tone=link)
The id of a note is `fn-` and its label, so a link to a note survives a renumbering. The id of a reference is `fnref-` and its number:

```md result
## Fn 1

Text.[^1]

[^1]: A note.
```

```js config
mdsvex({ heading_ids: true });
```

```md result config
## Fn 1

Text.[^1]

[^1]: A note.
```

:::notes
With [`heading_ids`](/docs/reference/options#heading_ids), a heading keeps its id, and a note whose id a heading took moves aside instead: `## Fn 1` keeps `fn-1` and the note `[^1]` becomes `fn-1-1`.
:::
::::

::::plate[mdsvex adds no CSS](mark=".css", tone=other)
mdsvex adds no CSS and no heading. The section has the `footnotes` class and an accessible name.

:::notes
The arrow of a back link is followed by a variation selector, so it is drawn as text and not as an emoji.
:::
::::

::::plate[A `[^…]` that is not a footnote](mark="\[^", tone=text)
A reference is read by its syntax alone, before any definition is known. So a `[^…]` in prose that is not a footnote, such as the regular expression class `[^abc]`, has to be escaped or put in code:

```md result
The class \[^abc] matches, and so does `[^abc]`.
```
::::

## Footnote errors

::::plate[A `FootnoteError`](mark="[^?]", tone=link)
A reference with no definition, a label defined twice and a definition with no text fail the build with a `FootnoteError`. It has the `code` (`footnote_undefined`, `footnote_duplicate` or `footnote_empty`), the `label`, and the `line` and `column` of the reference or of the definition, and `problems` lists every one in the document:

```md error file="src/routes/post.svx"
A claim[^nope].
```

```md error
A claim[^a].

[^a]: The source.
[^a]: Another source.
```

```md error
A claim[^a].

[^a]:
```

:::notes
A definition that nothing refers to is not rendered, and the compile warns with the code `footnote_unused`.
:::
::::

## Footnote options

::::plate[`label` and `back_label`](mark="{n}", tone=link)
`label` names the section and `back_label` each link back, where `{n}` is the number of the reference.

```js config
mdsvex({
	footnotes: {
		label: 'Notes',
		back_label: 'Back to note {n}'
	}
});
```

```md result config
A claim[^1].

[^1]: The source.
```
::::

::::plate[`strict: false`](mark="false", tone=text)
`strict: false` turns the two errors into warnings: a reference with no definition is numbered and linked all the same, and the first definition of a label is the one that renders. That suits a preview of a document that is still being written.
::::

## Footnote components

Two exports of a [components module](/docs/guides/components) replace footnotes:

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as footnote } from './markdown/Footnote.svelte';
export { default as footnotes } from './markdown/Footnotes.svelte';
```

::::plate[`footnote`](mark="<sup>", tone=comp)
`footnote` replaces a reference. It gets these props:

| prop | value |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | the link it renders as without a replacement, the `<sup>` |
| `note` | a snippet of what the definition holds |
| `label` | the label, as written |
| `number` | its number |
| `href` | the link to the note, `#fn-` and the label |
| `id` | the id of the reference, `fnref-` and the number |
::::

::::plate[A note in a hover card](mark="note", tone=comp)
`note` is what a side note or a hover card needs. A note holds blocks, and HTML allows no block inside the paragraph a reference is in, so draw it once the page is in the browser, as this card does on hover, or somewhere outside the paragraph:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Footnote.svelte -->
<script>
	let { children, note } = $props();

	let open = $state(false);
</script>

<span
	class="footnote"
	role="group"
	onpointerenter={() => (open = true)}
	onpointerleave={() => (open = false)}
>
	{@render children()}{#if open}<span class="card">{@render note()}</span>{/if}
</span>
```
::::

::::plate[`footnotes`](mark="<ol>", tone=comp)
`footnotes` replaces the section and gets the `<ol>` of notes as its `children`:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Footnotes.svelte -->
<script>
	let { children } = $props();
</script>

<aside class="notes" aria-label="Notes">
	<h2>Notes</h2>
	{@render children()}
</aside>
```

:::edges
- The links and the list that mdsvex writes for footnotes are not markdown, so `a`, `sup`, `ol` and `li` replacements never get them. What a note holds is markdown, so its paragraphs and links are replaced as anywhere else.
- A reference inside a `note` snippet gets no snippet of its own.
:::
::::

See the [footnotes playground example](/playground/footnotes).

## Footnotes while streaming

`compile` knows the whole document, so it can number the notes and move them. The streaming renderer does not: a reference shows its label as a superscript, and a definition renders where it is written, behind the same label.
